Then, since \displaystyle\int \frac{r\sec^2{\theta}
\hbox{d}\theta}{(r^2(1+\tan^2{\theta}))^{(3/2)}} = \displaystyle\int
\frac{r\sec^2{\theta} \hbox{d}\theta}{(r^2(1+\tan^2{\theta}))(r^2(1 +
\tan^2{\theta}))^{(1/2)}} = \displaystyle\int
\frac{\hbox{d}\theta}{r^2\sec{\theta}} = \frac{1}{r^2} \sin{\theta}
using a trig identity.

