static.solution:
T(x) = f(a) + \frac{f'(a)}{1!} (x-a) + \frac{f''(a)}{2!} (x-a)^2 + ... + \frac{f^n(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n



f'(x) = e^{x}
f''(x) = e^{x}



So, in this case, 



T_2(x) = e^{0.2}+e^{0.2}\mathopen{}\left(x-0.2\right)+\frac{e^{0.2}}{2}\mathopen{}\left(x-0.2\right)^{2}



|e^{x} - T_2(x)| = \left|e^{0.2}+e^{0.2}\mathopen{}\left(-1.5\right)+\frac{e^{0.2}}{2}\cdot 2.25-e^{-1.3}\right| = 0.490845

statement.p:
Note that when the constant in the denominator is not 1, we still can use the geometric series, but we have to multiply by
a form of 1 that helps us.  We'll use \frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\frac{1}{2}}.
\frac{1}{(2+x)}=\frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\frac{1}{2}(2+x)}=\frac{\frac{1}{2}}{1+\frac{1}{2}x}=\frac{1}{2}\cdot \frac{1}{1+\frac{1}{2}x}

static.solution:
\displaystyle c_n = \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}, e.g. 



\displaystyle c_0 = \frac{f(0)}{0!} = 0. 



\displaystyle c_1 = \frac{f'(0)}{1!}; 



\displaystyle f'(x)=6 \arctan(7 x) + 6 x\frac{1}{(7 x)^2+1}7 
= 6 \arctan(7 x) + \frac{42 x}{49 x^2+1}; 



c_1 = 0.



\displaystyle c_2 = \frac{f''(0)}{2!}; 



\displaystyle 
f''(x) = \left( 6 \arctan(7 x) + \frac{42 x}{49 x^2+1} \right)' 



\displaystyle = 
\frac{42}{49 x^2+1} + \frac{42(49 x^2+1)-42 x49\cdot 2x}{(49 x^2+1)^2}



\displaystyle
= \frac{2058 x^2 + 42}{(49 x^2+1)^2} + \frac{42 - 2058 x^2}{(49 x^2+1)^2}
= \frac{84}{(49 x^2+1)^2};



\displaystyle c_2 = \frac{84}{2} = 42;



and so on. Another way is to use the power series for arctan:  



\displaystyle \arctan(t) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n \frac{t^{2n+1}}{2n+1} 
= t-\frac{t^3}{3}+\frac{t^5}{5}-\frac{t^7}{7}+....



Then 



\displaystyle 6 x \arctan(7 x) = 
6 x \sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{(7 x)^{2n+1}}{2n+1} = 
\sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{6\cdot 7^{2n+1}}{2n+1} x^{2n+2}
\displaystyle  
= 42 x^2 - \frac{2058}{3} x^4 + \frac{100842}{5} x^6 - \frac{4941258}{7} x^7+.... 



So c_0=0, c_1=0, c_2=42, c_3=0, c_4=-\frac{2058}{3}=-686...



This is an alternating series. It converges if and only if 
\displaystyle \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{6 \cdot (7 x)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}=0. 
This limit is equal to 0 if and only if |7 x| \le 1, 
so \displaystyle |x|\le \frac{1}{7}. 
Therefore the radius of convergence is \displaystyle \frac{1}{7}.
